IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cla-nouns`

-- Civil Law Act 1909 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation (version in force from 15/10/2024)
-- LAYER 1 -- section 37: the standard of care for medical advice.
--
-- The illustrations to subsections (2) and (3) state outcomes in the Act's own words; each is encoded as a branch of the function it
-- illustrates and asserted as a test (fork F-I: if the illustrations are read as not operative, the same cases are the tests).
-- "Reasonable justification" (s 37(2)(b)) is not defined: only the two grounds the illustrations give and the one they refuse are decided.
-- Reading a result: JUST TRUE / JUST FALSE are answers the Act gives in its own words; NOTHING is "the Act does not answer".

§ `Section 37(1) -- the standard of care`

-- src:2058 | 37.—(1) A healthcare professional meets the standard of care in
-- src:2059 | relation to the provision of medical advice to a patient if —
-- src:2061 | (a) subject to subsection (2), the manner in which the
-- src:2062 | healthcare professional acts in the matter (at the time the
-- src:2063 | medical advice is provided) is accepted by a respectable
-- src:2064 | body of medical opinion (called in this section the peer
-- src:2065 | professional opinion) as reasonable professional practice
-- src:2066 | in the circumstances; and
-- src:2068 | (b) the peer professional opinion is logical.
@ref s 37(1) (src:2058-2068)
GIVEN `the manner in which the healthcare professional acts in the matter at the time the medical advice is provided is accepted by a respectable body of medical opinion as reasonable professional practice in the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peer professional opinion can be relied on under subsection (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peer professional opinion is logical` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(1) the healthcare professional meets the standard of care in relation to the provision of medical advice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the manner in which the healthcare professional acts in the matter at the time the medical advice is provided is accepted by a respectable body of medical opinion as reasonable professional practice in the circumstances` AND `the peer professional opinion can be relied on under subsection (2)` AND `the peer professional opinion is logical` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A healthcare professional meets the standard of care in relation to the provision of medical advice to a patient if — (a) subject to subsection (2), the manner in which the healthcare professional acts in the matter ... is accepted by a respectable body of medical opinion ... as reasonable professional practice in the circumstances; and (b) the peer professional opinion is logical" -- MARGINAL (a): a failed "if" is the text's no

§ `Section 37(2) -- when the peer professional opinion can be relied on`

-- src:2070 | (2) In order for the peer professional opinion mentioned in
-- src:2071 | subsection (1) to be relied on for the purposes of that subsection,
-- src:2072 | the peer professional opinion must —
-- src:2074 | (a) require the healthcare professional to have given (or
-- src:2075 | caused to be given) to the patient —
-- src:2077 | (i) information that a person in the same circumstances
-- src:2078 | as the patient (which circumstances the healthcare
-- src:2083 | professional knows or ought reasonably to know)
-- src:2084 | would reasonably require to make an informed
-- src:2085 | decision about whether to undergo a treatment or
-- src:2086 | follow a medical advice; and
-- src:2088 | (ii) information that the healthcare professional knows
-- src:2089 | or ought reasonably to know (in accordance with
-- src:2090 | subsection (3)) is material to the patient for the
-- src:2091 | purpose of making an informed decision about
-- src:2092 | whether to undergo the treatment or follow the
-- src:2093 | medical advice; and
-- src:2102 | (b) support the non-provision of any information mentioned in
-- src:2103 | paragraph (a)(i) or (ii) to the patient only where there is
-- src:2104 | reasonable justification for that.
@ref s 37(2) (src:2070-2104)
GIVEN `the peer professional opinion requires the healthcare professional to have given or caused to be given to the patient the information that a person in the same circumstances as the patient would reasonably require` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peer professional opinion requires the healthcare professional to have given or caused to be given to the patient the information that the healthcare professional knows or ought reasonably to know is material to the patient` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peer professional opinion supports the non-provision of that information to the patient only where there is reasonable justification` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(2) the peer professional opinion can be relied on for the purposes of subsection (1)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the peer professional opinion requires the healthcare professional to have given or caused to be given to the patient the information that a person in the same circumstances as the patient would reasonably require` AND `the peer professional opinion requires the healthcare professional to have given or caused to be given to the patient the information that the healthcare professional knows or ought reasonably to know is material to the patient` AND `the peer professional opinion supports the non-provision of that information to the patient only where there is reasonable justification` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "In order for the peer professional opinion mentioned in subsection (1) to be relied on for the purposes of that subsection, the peer professional opinion must — (a) require the healthcare professional to have given (or caused to be given) to the patient — (i) information that a person in the same circumstances as the patient ... would reasonably require ...; and (ii) information that the healthcare professional knows or ought reasonably to know ... is material to the patient ...; and (b) support the non-provision of any information ... only where there is reasonable justification for that"

-- src:2095 | Explanation. — Sub-paragraph (ii) refers to information which a person
-- src:2096 | in the same circumstances as the patient would not reasonably require to
-- src:2097 | make an informed decision (about whether to undergo a treatment or follow
-- src:2098 | a medical advice), but which is important to the patient, for the patient’s own
-- src:2099 | reason (including an idiosyncratic reason), for the purpose of making an
-- src:2100 | informed decision.
@ref s 37(2)(a)(ii) Explanation (src:2095-2100)
GIVEN `a person in the same circumstances as the patient would not reasonably require the information to make an informed decision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the information is important to the patient for the patient's own reason including an idiosyncratic reason for the purpose of making an informed decision` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(2)(a)(ii) the information is information to which sub-paragraph (ii) refers` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `a person in the same circumstances as the patient would not reasonably require the information to make an informed decision` AND `the information is important to the patient for the patient's own reason including an idiosyncratic reason for the purpose of making an informed decision` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "Sub-paragraph (ii) refers to information which a person in the same circumstances as the patient would not reasonably require ..., but which is important to the patient, for the patient's own reason" -- "refers to" names a case and does not close the sub-paragraph

-- src:2102 | (b) support the non-provision of any information mentioned in
-- src:2103 | paragraph (a)(i) or (ii) to the patient only where there is
-- src:2104 | reasonable justification for that.
-- src:2108 | (a) A (a doctor) decides to perform an emergency life-saving surgery on B
-- src:2109 | (a patient), who is unconscious, to create a temporary airway for B. Without
-- src:2110 | this surgery, B will not be able to breathe and will die. At the material time,
-- src:2111 | there is no person present with legal capacity to make medical decisions on
-- src:2112 | behalf of B, and insufficient time to locate or appoint such a person. A has
-- src:2113 | reasonable justification for not providing information about the surgery to B,
-- src:2114 | or any person with legal capacity to make medical decisions on behalf of B,
-- src:2115 | before performing the surgery.
-- src:2117 | (b) B (a patient) seeks treatment for breast cancer from A (a doctor) and
-- src:2118 | informs A that B would like to proceed with a lumpectomy. B also informs
-- src:2119 | that B had earlier consulted 2 other breast cancer specialists who had advised
-- src:2120 | B of the other treatment options (including their benefits and risks) available
-- src:2121 | to B, and expressly tells A that B does not want to be given this information
-- src:2122 | again. A is satisfied that B appreciates the seriousness of B’s decision to
-- src:2123 | waive B’s right to hear such information. A has reasonable justification for
-- src:2124 | not providing such information to B.
-- src:2126 | (c) A (a doctor) is of the view that it is in the best interests of B (a patient)
-- src:2127 | to undergo a procedure and that informing B about the risks of the procedure
-- src:2128 | would dissuade B from undergoing it. This, by itself, does not give A
-- src:2129 | reasonable justification for not informing B about the risks.
@ref s 37(2)(b) and its illustrations (src:2102-2129)
GIVEN `the surgery is emergency life-saving surgery on an unconscious patient and no person with legal capacity to make medical decisions on the patient's behalf is present and there is insufficient time to locate or appoint one` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the patient has been informed of the information by others and expressly tells the healthcare professional that the patient does not want it again and the healthcare professional is satisfied that the patient appreciates the seriousness of waiving it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the only ground is the healthcare professional's view that informing the patient of the risks would dissuade the patient from undergoing a procedure that is in the patient's best interests` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(2)(b) there is reasonable justification for the non-provision of the information` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the surgery is emergency life-saving surgery on an unconscious patient and no person with legal capacity to make medical decisions on the patient's behalf is present and there is insufficient time to locate or appoint one` THEN JUST TRUE -- "A has reasonable justification" (illustration (a))
        IF `the patient has been informed of the information by others and expressly tells the healthcare professional that the patient does not want it again and the healthcare professional is satisfied that the patient appreciates the seriousness of waiving it` THEN JUST TRUE -- "A has reasonable justification for not providing such information to B" (illustration (b))
        IF `the only ground is the healthcare professional's view that informing the patient of the risks would dissuade the patient from undergoing a procedure that is in the patient's best interests` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "This, by itself, does not give A reasonable justification for not informing B about the risks" (illustration (c))
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "reasonable justification" is not defined; the illustrations decide three grounds and no more

§ `Section 37(3) and (4) -- materiality; differing opinions`

-- src:2134 | (3) In subsection (2)(a)(ii), an assessment as to whether any
-- src:2135 | information is material to the patient for the purpose of making an
-- src:2136 | informed decision about whether to undergo a treatment or follow a
-- src:2137 | medical advice must be based on any specific concern or query the
-- src:2138 | patient has in relation to the treatment or medical advice —
-- src:2140 | (a) which the patient expressly communicates to the
-- src:2141 | healthcare professional; or
-- src:2145 | B (a patient) asks A (B’s doctor) about the risks of influenza vaccination. B
-- src:2146 | expresses a concern to A about side effects of weakness or paralysis after
-- src:2147 | being administered the influenza vaccine (which alludes to the Guillain-
-- src:2148 | Barré syndrome). B is not a high-risk patient for developing Guillain-Barré
-- src:2149 | syndrome from influenza vaccination, and doctors would not ordinarily
-- src:2150 | advise a patient in B’s circumstances about Guillain-Barré syndrome as it is
-- src:2151 | a very rare side effect of the influenza vaccine. However, since B has
-- src:2152 | expressly communicated such a concern to A, information on the risks of
-- src:2153 | Guillain-Barré syndrome is material to B for the purpose of B making an
-- src:2154 | informed decision about whether to receive influenza vaccination.
-- src:2156 | (b) which the patient does not expressly communicate to the
-- src:2157 | healthcare professional but which ought to be apparent to
-- src:2158 | the healthcare professional from the patient’s medical
-- src:2159 | records that the healthcare professional has reasonable
-- src:2160 | access to and ought reasonably to review.
-- src:2164 | Slightly dry skin at the surgery site is a rare but possible consequence of
-- src:2165 | knee replacement surgery. The general medical opinion is that a patient
-- src:2166 | would not reasonably require information about the possibility of dry skin at
-- src:2167 | the surgery site in order to make an informed decision as to whether to
-- src:2168 | undergo knee replacement surgery.
-- src:2170 | B (a patient) consults A (a doctor) on knee replacement surgery. B has an
-- src:2171 | unusual concern that B’s skin should not become dry, but does not expressly
-- src:2172 | communicate this concern to A. A year earlier, B had consulted C (another
-- src:2173 | doctor working in the same hospital as A) on knee replacement surgery but
-- src:2174 | did not proceed with it. B’s medical records containing notes of B’s
-- src:2175 | consultation with C show that B had repeatedly asked C about dry skin and
-- src:2176 | stated that B did not want the skin on B’s knee to feel dry after such surgery.
-- src:2177 | Therefore, it ought to be apparent to A from such medical records of B that
-- src:2178 | information about dry skin being a possible consequence of knee
-- src:2179 | replacement surgery is material to B for the purpose of B making an
-- src:2180 | informed decision about whether to undergo the surgery.
@ref s 37(3) (src:2134-2180)
GIVEN `the information addresses a specific concern or query the patient has in relation to the treatment or medical advice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the patient expressly communicates the concern or query to the healthcare professional` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the concern or query ought to be apparent to the healthcare professional from the patient's medical records that the healthcare professional has reasonable access to and ought reasonably to review` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(3) the information is material to the patient for the purpose of making an informed decision` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the information addresses a specific concern or query the patient has in relation to the treatment or medical advice` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any specific concern or query the patient has in relation to the treatment or medical advice"
        IF `the patient expressly communicates the concern or query to the healthcare professional` OR `the concern or query ought to be apparent to the healthcare professional from the patient's medical records that the healthcare professional has reasonable access to and ought reasonably to review` THEN JUST TRUE -- "information on the risks of Guillain-Barré syndrome is material to B" (illustration to (a)); "information about dry skin ... is material to B" (illustration to (b)) -- fork F-I2: the subsection says the assessment "must be based on" such a concern; the illustrations say the information is material
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a concern neither communicated nor apparent from the records: a bare "must be based on" (fork QB)

-- src:2185 | (4) The fact that there are differing professional opinions held by
-- src:2186 | other respected healthcare professionals does not, by itself, prevent
-- src:2187 | the peer professional opinion mentioned in subsection (1) from being
-- src:2188 | relied on for the purpose of that subsection.
@ref s 37(4) (src:2185-2188)
GIVEN `the only ground relied on against the peer professional opinion is that there are differing professional opinions held by other respected healthcare professionals` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(4) the differing professional opinions prevent the peer professional opinion from being relied on` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the only ground relied on against the peer professional opinion is that there are differing professional opinions held by other respected healthcare professionals` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The fact that there are differing professional opinions held by other respected healthcare professionals does not, by itself, prevent the peer professional opinion mentioned in subsection (1) from being relied on"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "by itself"

§ `Section 37(5) to (7) -- logical; patient; healthcare professional`

-- src:2190 | (5) For the purposes of subsection (1)(b), a peer professional
-- src:2191 | opinion is logical where —
-- src:2193 | (a) the body of healthcare professionals holding the opinion
-- src:2194 | has directed its mind to the comparative risks and benefits
-- src:2195 | relating to the matter; and
-- src:2197 | (b) the opinion is internally consistent and does not contradict
-- src:2198 | proven extrinsic facts relevant to the matter.
@ref s 37(5) (src:2190-2198)
GIVEN `the body of healthcare professionals holding the opinion has directed its mind to the comparative risks and benefits relating to the matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the opinion is internally consistent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the opinion does not contradict proven extrinsic facts relevant to the matter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(5) the peer professional opinion is logical` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the body of healthcare professionals holding the opinion has directed its mind to the comparative risks and benefits relating to the matter` AND `the opinion is internally consistent` AND `the opinion does not contradict proven extrinsic facts relevant to the matter` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a peer professional opinion is logical where — (a) the body of healthcare professionals holding the opinion has directed its mind to the comparative risks and benefits relating to the matter; and (b) the opinion is internally consistent and does not contradict proven extrinsic facts relevant to the matter" -- MARGINAL (a): a failed "where" is the text's no (ruling of 2026-11-05, rule (b))

-- src:2200 | (6) A reference in this section to a patient includes, in the case
-- src:2201 | where the patient is under a legal disability, a reference to a person
-- src:2202 | with the responsibility for making a decision for the patient having
-- src:2203 | regard to medical advice.
@ref s 37(6) (src:2200-2203)
GIVEN `the patient is under a legal disability` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has the responsibility for making a decision for the patient having regard to medical advice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(6) the person is a patient for the purposes of section 37` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the patient is under a legal disability` AND `the person has the responsibility for making a decision for the patient having regard to medical advice` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "A reference in this section to a patient includes, in the case where the patient is under a legal disability, a reference to a person with the responsibility for making a decision for the patient"

-- src:2205 | (7) In this section, “healthcare professional” means an individual
-- src:2206 | who practises a profession that provides medical advice, and
-- src:2207 | includes —
-- src:2209 | (a) a medical practitioner registered (or deemed to be
-- src:2210 | registered) under the Medical Registration Act 1997*; and
-- src:2212 | (b) a dentist registered (or deemed to be registered), and an
-- src:2213 | oral health therapist registered, under the Dental
-- src:2214 | Registration Act 1999†.
@ref s 37(7) (src:2205-2214)
GIVEN `the person is an individual who practises a profession that provides medical advice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a medical practitioner registered or deemed to be registered under the Medical Registration Act 1997` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a dentist registered or deemed to be registered or an oral health therapist registered under the Dental Registration Act 1999` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37(7) the individual is a healthcare professional` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the person is an individual who practises a profession that provides medical advice` OR `the person is a medical practitioner registered or deemed to be registered under the Medical Registration Act 1997` OR `the person is a dentist registered or deemed to be registered or an oral health therapist registered under the Dental Registration Act 1999` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“healthcare professional” means an individual who practises a profession that provides medical advice, and includes — (a) a medical practitioner ...; and (b) a dentist ... and an oral health therapist" -- "means ... and includes" is read as closing the class at the two limbs (fork F-D1)

-- Hand-gathering function (ruling E1, option B as guidance): the standard of care from the plain facts, reading (2) and (5) before (1).
@ref s 37 gathered (src:2058-2198)
GIVEN `the manner in which the healthcare professional acts in the matter at the time the medical advice is provided is accepted by a respectable body of medical opinion as reasonable professional practice in the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peer professional opinion requires the healthcare professional to have given or caused to be given to the patient the information that a person in the same circumstances as the patient would reasonably require` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peer professional opinion requires the healthcare professional to have given or caused to be given to the patient the information that the healthcare professional knows or ought reasonably to know is material to the patient` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peer professional opinion supports the non-provision of that information to the patient only where there is reasonable justification` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the body of healthcare professionals holding the opinion has directed its mind to the comparative risks and benefits relating to the matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the opinion is internally consistent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the opinion does not contradict proven extrinsic facts relevant to the matter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 37 the healthcare professional meets the standard of care` MEANS
    `s 37(1) the healthcare professional meets the standard of care in relation to the provision of medical advice`
        `the manner in which the healthcare professional acts in the matter at the time the medical advice is provided is accepted by a respectable body of medical opinion as reasonable professional practice in the circumstances`
        (`s 37(2) the peer professional opinion can be relied on for the purposes of subsection (1)` `the peer professional opinion requires the healthcare professional to have given or caused to be given to the patient the information that a person in the same circumstances as the patient would reasonably require` `the peer professional opinion requires the healthcare professional to have given or caused to be given to the patient the information that the healthcare professional knows or ought reasonably to know is material to the patient` `the peer professional opinion supports the non-provision of that information to the patient only where there is reasonable justification` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
        (`s 37(5) the peer professional opinion is logical` `the body of healthcare professionals holding the opinion has directed its mind to the comparative risks and benefits relating to the matter` `the opinion is internally consistent` `the opinion does not contradict proven extrinsic facts relevant to the matter` EQUALS JUST TRUE)

§§ `Section 37 -- tests`

#ASSERT `s 37(1) the healthcare professional meets the standard of care in relation to the provision of medical advice` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- "meets the standard of care"
#ASSERT `s 37(1) the healthcare professional meets the standard of care in relation to the provision of medical advice` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- (a) not accepted by a respectable body: "if"
#ASSERT `s 37(1) the healthcare professional meets the standard of care in relation to the provision of medical advice` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- "subject to subsection (2)": the opinion cannot be relied on
#ASSERT `s 37(1) the healthcare professional meets the standard of care in relation to the provision of medical advice` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- (b) not logical
#ASSERT `s 37(2) the peer professional opinion can be relied on for the purposes of subsection (1)` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- (a)(i), (a)(ii) and (b)
#ASSERT `s 37(2) the peer professional opinion can be relied on for the purposes of subsection (1)` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- (a)(i) missing: "In order for ... must"
#ASSERT `s 37(2) the peer professional opinion can be relied on for the purposes of subsection (1)` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- (a)(ii) missing
#ASSERT `s 37(2) the peer professional opinion can be relied on for the purposes of subsection (1)` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- (b) missing
#ASSERT `s 37(2)(a)(ii) the information is information to which sub-paragraph (ii) refers` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- the Explanation: not required by a person in the same circumstances, but important to this patient
#ASSERT `s 37(2)(a)(ii) the information is information to which sub-paragraph (ii) refers` FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING -- "refers to" does not close the sub-paragraph
#ASSERT `s 37(2)(a)(ii) the information is information to which sub-paragraph (ii) refers` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING -- not important to the patient
-- the illustrations to (2)(b)
#ASSERT `s 37(2)(b) there is reasonable justification for the non-provision of the information` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- illustration (a): the emergency airway surgery
#ASSERT `s 37(2)(b) there is reasonable justification for the non-provision of the information` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- illustration (b): the patient waived information given earlier by two other specialists
#ASSERT `s 37(2)(b) there is reasonable justification for the non-provision of the information` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- illustration (c): informing would dissuade the patient, by itself
#ASSERT `s 37(2)(b) there is reasonable justification for the non-provision of the information` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING -- "reasonable justification" is not defined
#ASSERT `s 37(2)(b) there is reasonable justification for the non-provision of the information` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- an emergency stands even where the doctor also thinks the patient would be dissuaded
-- the illustrations to (3)
#ASSERT `s 37(3) the information is material to the patient for the purpose of making an informed decision` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- illustration to (a): B expressly asks about weakness or paralysis after influenza vaccination
#ASSERT `s 37(3) the information is material to the patient for the purpose of making an informed decision` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- illustration to (b): B's records show repeated questions about dry skin
#ASSERT `s 37(3) the information is material to the patient for the purpose of making an informed decision` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING -- a concern neither communicated nor apparent from the records
#ASSERT `s 37(3) the information is material to the patient for the purpose of making an informed decision` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING -- the information does not address the patient's concern
-- (4) to (7)
#ASSERT `s 37(4) the differing professional opinions prevent the peer professional opinion from being relied on` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- "does not, by itself, prevent"
#ASSERT `s 37(4) the differing professional opinions prevent the peer professional opinion from being relied on` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING -- "by itself": another ground may prevent it
#ASSERT `s 37(5) the peer professional opinion is logical` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- (a) and (b)
#ASSERT `s 37(5) the peer professional opinion is logical` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- (a) not met: "where"
#ASSERT `s 37(5) the peer professional opinion is logical` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- (b) not internally consistent
#ASSERT `s 37(5) the peer professional opinion is logical` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- (b) contradicts proven extrinsic facts
#ASSERT `s 37(6) the person is a patient for the purposes of section 37` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- "includes ... a person with the responsibility for making a decision for the patient"
#ASSERT `s 37(6) the person is a patient for the purposes of section 37` FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING -- the patient is not under a legal disability
#ASSERT `s 37(7) the individual is a healthcare professional` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- "means an individual who practises a profession that provides medical advice"
#ASSERT `s 37(7) the individual is a healthcare professional` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- (a) a registered medical practitioner
#ASSERT `s 37(7) the individual is a healthcare professional` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- (b) a registered dentist or oral health therapist
#ASSERT `s 37(7) the individual is a healthcare professional` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- "means" (fork F-D1)
-- gathered
#ASSERT `s 37 the healthcare professional meets the standard of care` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE -- every limb met
#ASSERT `s 37 the healthcare professional meets the standard of care` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- the opinion does not support non-provision only on reasonable justification
#ASSERT `s 37 the healthcare professional meets the standard of care` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE -- the opinion is not internally consistent
